From 924fa50b9e2ba16d0293cd3f1bc15c4fdc618141 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 08:32:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Water that answers what moves through it: wakes, rings and
 splashes under the new look

Corey's reference (a retail pond seen from above) shows what our water
lacked: a swimmer leaves a crisp white wake fanning out behind him in a
V, a hero wading in sends rings out and splashes where he steps in, the
bed wavers under the moving surface, the shore thins to clear water with
a faint pale edge, and the shallows show their sand plainly.

The wakes. A ripple field of 256 x 256 texels over 64 yd round the
camera (a little ahead of it, where the view falls), snapped to whole
texels, is stepped on the GPU each frame (look_wakes.wgsl, one compute
pass, look_wakes in the fps journal's gpu_look column and named on its
GPU_PASSES line) as a linear shallow-water wave at fixed 1/120 s steps,
in pairs. Every unit with its feet in the water pushes on it by its
speed, smoothed over 0.12 s (a still one only breathes gentle rings),
one coming in splashes, a fishing bobber nods, and raindrops dimple it.

The V. Behind a moving body two arms trail from its sides at 19.5
degrees either side of its track, as a Kelvin wake's do at any speed,
reaching back as far as it goes in 1.2 s. They push on the water and lay
foam along them, and their foam is carried outward as fast as the arms
move, so it stays on them as a crisp V of white streaks that fades over
about a second and a half. Foam is born only there, at the body's sides
and as a splash's few droplets on a ring round it: a still body, a ring
or a raindrop never foams, a texel takes at most 12 a second, and new
foam is held back as the share of the field under it nears 4% (read
back from the GPU). The water draws it white, crisp where it is thick
and a soft veil where it is thin, so it fades rather than blinks out.
The field's slopes add to the water's own ripples, so the mirror, the
glint and the bed move with it.

They replace 0081's wading froth and 1.12's own wade wake and rings
(water_fx), which drew an older ring over them: the ring round a
standing hero's neck that four films found is 1.12's, and it no longer
draws while the wakes run. `lookwater wakes 0` brings both back.

The bed through moving water: the bed seen under the water is drawn back
from the surface's tilt, 3.5 yd per unit of slope per yard of depth to
1.5 yd deep, so it wavers under the ripples and the wakes; faint moving
light lies on the shallow bed in the sun. Where the bend is under a
pixel the frame's blend keeps the bed it already holds, so a calm
surface still shows what is drawn behind it.

The shore and the shallows, retuned toward the reference: no foam line,
fully clear at the waterline with a faint pale haze over its first
0.7 yd, clearer water (1.8 yd to a third, was 1.4), a greener shallow
colour that holds to 1.2 yd and turns to the deep by 3.5.

Switches: worldWater 0 and worldLook 0 run 1.12's water and wade foam
untouched. `lookwater wakes [0|1]` (with no value it reports the bodies
pushing, the field's size, the share of it under foam read back from the
GPU, and the fastest body's speed and depth; the same line is logged
every 10 s while something moves the water), `lookwater refract <0-2>`
and `lookwater caustics <0-2>` hold the bend and the light, `grade` lets
go.

